<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>How loud is too loud for headphones?</h3>
<p>As a rule of thumb, aim for the quietest level that’s clearly enjoyable. Public‑health guidance often uses 85 dB as a maximum for about 8 hours, with safe time halving every 3 dB increase. Because exact dB at your eardrum is hard to know, protect yourself by turning on volume limits, using ANC/isolation so you don’t need high levels, and taking listening breaks. If your ears ring after listening, it was likely too loud or too long.</p>

<h3>Are noise‑canceling headphones safer?</h3>
<p>They can be. ANC reduces background noise, especially low‑frequency rumble, so you can listen at a lower volume for the same clarity. The safer choice is ANC plus a good seal, with volume limits enabled and loudness normalization turned on.</p>

<h3>Can EQ increase the risk of hearing damage?</h3>
<p>EQ doesn’t increase risk by itself. Risk comes from loudness and time. In fact, gentle EQ can make audio more satisfying at lower volumes—like adding a light bass shelf so you don’t feel tempted to crank it. Keep boosts modest, avoid clipping, and combine with isolation/ANC.</p>

<h3>Are bone‑conduction headphones safer than earbuds?</h3>
<p>Not automatically. They leave your ears open, which is great for awareness, but in noisy places you may still turn them up. In very loud environments, consider earplugs for protection and keep headphone volume modest. Situational awareness doesn’t replace safe listening levels.</p>


